problem:
Problem description
- There is a planning horizon of $$T$$ periods, indexed by $$t \in \mathcal{T} = \{1,\dots,T\}$$.
- In each period $$t$$, a quantity of fighter jets is produced and becomes available for allocation.
- A decision is made in each period about how many of the available fighter jets are dedicated exclusively to pilot training.
- Each training jet can train up to $$\kappa$$ pilots per period.
- A trained pilot can operate one combat jet in later periods after training is completed.
- Training begins in period $$s$$ and is tracked over the planning horizon.
- If training cost information is included, let $$c_t$$ denote the cost of assigning one jet to training in period $$t$$.
- The planning goal is to determine a feasible training allocation that maximizes the number of trained pilots available by the end of the horizon for air defense.
- Business constraints:
  - Jets assigned to training in a period are reserved only for training in that period and are not simultaneously available for combat use in that same period.
  - The number of jets allocated to training in any period cannot exceed the jets produced and available in that period.
  - The number of pilots trained in any period cannot exceed the training capacity created by the number of training jets used in that period.
  - Pilots become available for combat only after completing training.
  - The total number of trained pilots available by the end of the horizon is the main quantity of interest.
- A solution is represented by:
  - $$x_t$$: the number of fighter jets allocated exclusively to training in period $$t$$.
  - $$z_t$$: the number of pilots trained in period $$t$$.
  - $$y_t$$: the cumulative number of trained pilots available by the end of period $$t$$.
  - If needed, a period-by-period record of how many jets remain available for combat after training allocation.

Input Format
{
  "time_horizon": {
    "symbol": "$$T$$",
    "meaning": "number of planning periods"
  },
  "period_index_set": {
    "symbol": "$$\mathcal{T}$$",
    "meaning": "set of periods indexed by $$t$$"
  },
  "production_by_period": {
    "symbol": "$$a_t$$",
    "shape": "[$$T$$]",
    "meaning": "number of fighter jets produced and available in period $$t$$"
  },
  "training_capacity_per_jet": {
    "symbol": "$$\kappa$$",
    "shape": "scalar",
    "meaning": "number of pilots that one training jet can train per period"
  },
  "training_start_period": {
    "symbol": "$$s$$",
    "shape": "scalar",
    "meaning": "first period in which training is allowed"
  },
  "availability_lag": {
    "symbol": "$$\delta$$",
    "shape": "scalar",
    "meaning": "number of periods after training before a pilot is available for combat"
  },
  "optional_training_cost_by_period": {
    "symbol": "$$c_t$$",
    "shape": "[$$T$$]",
    "meaning": "optional cost of dedicating one jet to training in period $$t$$"
  }
}

Output Format
{
  "training_allocation_by_period": {
    "symbol": "$$x_t$$",
    "shape": "[$$T$$]",
    "meaning": "number of fighter jets allocated exclusively to training in period $$t$$"
  },
  "pilots_trained_by_period": {
    "symbol": "$$z_t$$",
    "shape": "[$$T$$]",
    "meaning": "number of pilots trained in period $$t$$"
  },
  "trained_pilots_available_by_period": {
    "symbol": "$$y_t$$",
    "shape": "[$$T$$]",
    "meaning": "cumulative number of trained pilots available by the end of period $$t$$"
  },
  "total_trained_pilots": {
    "symbol": "$$y_T$$",
    "shape": "scalar",
    "meaning": "total number of trained pilots available by the end of the horizon"
  }
}

Example(s)

Input 1
{
  "time_horizon": "$$2$$",
  "production_by_period": [
    "$$10$$",
    "$$15$$"
  ],
  "training_capacity_per_jet": "$$5$$",
  "training_start_period": "$$1$$",
  "availability_lag": "$$1$$"
}
